# FABLE-SONNET-RECEIVE-RENAME-20260924-166 commit 3 (D-48): Q24 = A (author, LMX_blog/2026-09-25.md,
# q24.md) -- a repeated TYPED declaration of one name in one body is a new occurrence, the same
# mechanism the argument same-name-binding rule already uses. `test\[0]arg` = 1 (first), `test\[1]arg`
# = 2 (second), unqualified `test\arg` = 2 (last, Q22.2 = A, FABLE-SONNET-LAST-OCCURRENCE-20260924-164).
fn: test () int
    int: arg 1
    int: arg 2
    return: 0
end: test

# The author's own example (LMX_blog/2026-09-25.md): a repeated declaration's initialiser
# expression reads whatever occurrence was declared BEFORE this statement -- `x + 2` reads the
# first `x` (1), not the row this very declaration is creating -- giving 3, not 2 or 4.
fn: expr_init () int
    int: x 1
    int: x (x + 2)
    return: 0
end: expr_init

fn: check () int
    int: first
    int: second
    int: last
    test()
    first: test\[0]arg
    second: test\[1]arg
    last: test\arg
    if: first != 1
        return: 81
    if: second != 2
        return: 82
    if: last != 2
        return: 83
    test\arg: 5
    second: test\[1]arg
    first: test\[0]arg
    if: second != 5
        return: 84
    if: first != 1
        return: 85
    expr_init()
    first: expr_init\[0]x
    second: expr_init\[1]x
    if: first != 1
        return: 86
    if: second != 3
        return: 87
    return: 7
end: check

sendMessage: exit(exit_code: check(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
